# re.scup -- the regular expression language's grammar.
#
# TODO: replace the stub with the real grammar.  Remember from HW 3:
# no left recursion; one rule per precedence level (postfix operators
# bind tighter than concatenation '.', which binds tighter than
# alternation '|').  A %left block with a %binary rule builds a whole
# ladder of binary operators, and %chain(base, suffixRule) runs a
# postfix loop, binding $$ to the expression built so far.  Every
# atom alternative should start with a distinct terminal -- if two
# share a prefix, the LL(1) check names the conflicting productions
# when the grammar loads (`sbt "runMain scup.check re.scup"` checks
# it without running anything else).
#
# You must also handle let-bindings:
#
#   let X = c.o.w in X.X
#
# Grammar rules are static, so you cannot thread an environment
# through the parse.  Instead have your actions build a small
# intermediate tree of your own that still contains Let and Id nodes,
# and substitute each name's definition in a second "resolve" pass in
# REParser.scala -- so bindings never show up in your final AST.  An
# unbound name is an REError.

%{
  import re.ast.*
%}

%start  re
%type   re: RENode

# The stub grammar: a "regular expression" is a single letter.
re ::= LETTER  { REEmpty() /* TODO: surely not REEmpty... */ } ;
